<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-standard-normal" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Standard Normal Distribution</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Compute a <m>z</m>-score from a value, a mean, and a standard deviation.</p></li>
      <li><p>Interpret a <m>z</m>-score as a number of standard deviations above or below the mean.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recover an original value from a <m>z</m>-score.</p></li>
      <li><p>Apply the Empirical Rule to a normal distribution.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>Two exam scores, <m>72</m> and <m>58</m>, tell you almost nothing on
    their own. If the first exam had a mean of <m>85</m> and the second a mean
    of <m>45</m>, the lower raw score is the better performance. Comparing
    values across different distributions requires putting them on a common
    scale, and this section builds that scale.</p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-zscore">
    <title>Standardizing: the <m>z</m>-score</title>

    <p>The <term>standard normal distribution</term> is a normal distribution
    of standardized values called <term><m>z</m>-scores</term>. A
    <m>z</m>-score is measured in units of the standard deviation. If a
    distribution has mean <m>5</m> and standard deviation <m>2</m>, then the
    value <m>11</m> sits three standard deviations above the mean, since
    <m>11 = 5 + 3(2)</m>.</p>

    <definition xml:id="def-zscore">
      <title><m>z</m>-score</title>
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>X</m> is normally distributed with
        <m>X \sim N(\mu, \sigma)</m>, the <term><m>z</m>-score</term> of a
        value <m>x</m> is</p>
        <me>z = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma}</me>
        <p>The <m>z</m>-score reports how many standard deviations <m>x</m>
        lies above or below <m>\mu</m>. Values above the mean have
        <em>positive</em> <m>z</m>-scores; values below have <em>negative</em>
        <m>z</m>-scores. If <m>x = \mu</m>, then <m>z = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The transformation in <xref ref="def-zscore"/> produces a new
    distribution <m>Z \sim N(0, 1)</m><mdash/>mean <m>0</m>, standard
    deviation <m>1</m><mdash/>no matter what <m>\mu</m> and <m>\sigma</m>
    you started with.</p>

    <example xml:id="ex-zscore-basic">
      <title>Computing two <m>z</m>-scores</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose <m>X \sim N(5, 6)</m>, so <m>\mu = 5</m> and
        <m>\sigma = 6</m>. Find and interpret the <m>z</m>-scores for
        <m>x = 17</m> and <m>x = 1</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For <m>x = 17</m>:</p>
        <me>z = \frac{17 - 5}{6} = 2</me>
        <p>So <m>17</m> is two standard deviations to the right of the mean.
        Check by reversing: <m>5 + (2)(6) = 17</m>.</p>
        <p>For <m>x = 1</m>:</p>
        <me>z = \frac{1 - 5}{6} \approx -0.67</me>
        <p>So <m>1</m> is about <m>0.67</m> standard deviations to the left of
        the mean. Check: <m>5 + (-0.67)(6) \approx 1</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <!-- READING CHECK 1 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-zscore-sign">
      <statement>
        <p>A student computes a <m>z</m>-score of <m>z = -1.8</m> for her value.
        What does this tell her?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>Her value is <m>1.8</m> standard deviations <em>below</em>
            the mean.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. The negative sign places the value below the mean, and
            the size <m>1.8</m> counts standard deviations.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Her value is <m>1.8</m> standard deviations <em>above</em>
            the mean.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Check the sign. A negative <m>z</m>-score means the value is
            below the mean.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Her value is <m>1.8</m> units below the mean.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Close, but a <m>z</m>-score counts <em>standard deviations</em>,
            not the original units. If <m>\sigma = 10</m>, then
            <m>z = -1.8</m> means <m>18</m> units below the mean.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Her value is <m>1.8</m> times smaller than the mean.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>A <m>z</m>-score is not a ratio. It measures distance from the
            mean in units of <m>\sigma</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <example xml:id="ex-weight-loss">
      <title>Weight change</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Some doctors believe a person can lose five pounds on average in a
        month by reducing fat intake and exercising. Let <m>X</m> be the weight
        lost in pounds in one month, with <m>X \sim N(5, 2)</m>. Interpret the
        <m>z</m>-scores for a person who lost <m>10</m> pounds and a person who
        <em>gained</em> three pounds.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For a loss of <m>10</m> pounds, <m>x = 10</m>:</p>
        <me>z = \frac{10 - 5}{2} = 2.5</me>
        <p>This person is <m>2.5</m> standard deviations to the right of the
        mean.</p>
        <p>A gain of three pounds is a loss of <m>-3</m> pounds, so
        <m>x = -3</m>:</p>
        <me>z = \frac{-3 - 5}{2} = -4</me>
        <p>This person is four standard deviations to the left of the mean<mdash/>a
        genuinely unusual outcome.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <!-- READING CHECK 2 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-zscore-negative-x">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>In <xref ref="ex-weight-loss"/>, a negative value of <m>x</m> is
        meaningful: it represents weight <em>gained</em> rather than lost.</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Right. The variable records weight <em>lost</em>, so a negative
        amount lost is a gain. Always ask what the variable measures before
        deciding whether a value is possible.</p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-comparing">
    <title>Comparing values across distributions</title>

    <p>Suppose <m>X \sim N(5, 6)</m> and <m>Y \sim N(2, 1)</m>. We found that
    <m>x = 17</m> gives <m>z = 2</m>. For <m>y = 4</m>,</p>
    <me>z = \frac{4 - 2}{1} = 2</me>
    <p>Both values sit exactly two standard deviations to the right of their
    own means, even though <m>17</m> and <m>4</m> are nowhere near each other
    numerically. <em>This is what <m>z</m>-scores are for.</em> If <m>X</m>
    records weight gain for one group and <m>Y</m> records weight gain for a
    second group, then <m>x = 17</m> and <m>y = 4</m> represent the same
    standardized gain relative to their own groups.</p>

    <example xml:id="ex-chile-2009">
      <title>Heights in Chile, 2009<ndash/>2010</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The mean height of 15- to 18-year-old males from Chile in
        2009<ndash/>2010 was <m>170</m> cm with standard deviation
        <m>6.28</m> cm, so <m>X \sim N(170, 6.28)</m>.</p>
        <p>(a) Interpret the <m>z</m>-score for a male who is <m>168</m> cm tall.</p>
        <p>(b) A male has <m>z = 1.27</m>. How tall is he?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>(a) <m>z = \dfrac{168 - 170}{6.28} \approx -0.32</m>, so <m>168</m> cm
        is about <m>0.32</m> standard deviations below the mean.</p>
        <p>(b) Solving <m>z = \dfrac{x - \mu}{\sigma}</m> for <m>x</m> gives</p>
        <me>x = \mu + z\sigma</me>
        <p>so <m>x = 170 + (1.27)(6.28) = 177.98</m> cm.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <assemblage xml:id="asm-two-forms">
      <title>Two forms of one relationship</title>
      <p>From a value to a <m>z</m>-score:
      <m>z = \dfrac{x - \mu}{\sigma}</m>.</p>
      <p>From a <m>z</m>-score back to a value:
      <m>x = \mu + z\sigma</m>.</p>
      <p>These are the same equation. Which one you use depends on which
      quantity you are missing.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <!-- READING CHECK 3 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-which-formula">
      <statement>
        <p>A distribution has <m>\mu = 64</m> and <m>\sigma = 2.5</m>. You are
        told a value has <m>z</m>-score <m>z = -0.8</m> and asked for the
        value itself. Which computation gives the answer?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>64 + (-0.8)(2.5)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct<mdash/>this is <m>x = \mu + z\sigma</m>, giving
            <m>x = 62</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\dfrac{-0.8 - 64}{2.5}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is the standardizing formula, but you already have the
            <m>z</m>-score. You need to go the other direction.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>64 - \dfrac{0.8}{2.5}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The <m>z</m>-score is multiplied by <m>\sigma</m>, not divided
            by it.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>(-0.8)(64) + 2.5</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Review the box <xref ref="asm-two-forms" text="title"/>. The mean is
added, and the <m>z</m>-score multiplies the standard deviation.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <example xml:id="ex-chile-compare">
      <title>Comparing two generations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>From 1984 to 1985, the mean height of 15- to 18-year-old Chilean
        males was <m>172.36</m> cm with standard deviation <m>6.34</m> cm, so
        <m>Y \sim N(172.36, 6.34)</m>. Recall <m>X \sim N(170, 6.28)</m> for
        2009<ndash/>2010.</p>
        <p>Compare a 2009 male of height <m>160.58</m> cm with a 1984 male of
        height <m>162.85</m> cm.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <me>z_x = \frac{160.58 - 170}{6.28} = -1.5
          \qquad
          z_y = \frac{162.85 - 172.36}{6.34} = -1.5</me>
        <p>Both fall exactly <m>1.5</m> standard deviations below their own
        means. Relative to their own generations, these two heights are equally
        short<mdash/>even though the raw measurements differ by more than two
        centimeters.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <!-- READING CHECK 4: short answer, recorded -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-explain-comparison">
      <statement>
        <p>In <xref ref="ex-chile-compare"/>, the 1984 male is
        <em>taller</em> in centimeters than the 2009 male, yet both have the
        same <m>z</m>-score. In one or two sentences, explain to a classmate
        how both of those statements can be true at the same time.</p>
        <response/>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The two men are being measured against different reference groups.
        The 1984 population was taller on average
        (<m>\mu = 172.36</m> versus <m>\mu = 170</m>), so a taller raw height
        is needed to occupy the same relative position. A <m>z</m>-score
        describes position <em>within</em> a distribution, not absolute size,
        so identical <m>z</m>-scores mean identical standing in their
        respective populations.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-empirical-rule">
    <title>The Empirical Rule</title>

    <p>Because every normal distribution becomes the same standard normal
    distribution after standardizing, one set of percentages applies to all
    of them.</p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-empirical-rule">
      <title>The Empirical Rule</title>
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>X \sim N(\mu, \sigma)</m>, then approximately:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>68\%</m> of values lie between <m>\mu - \sigma</m> and
          <m>\mu + \sigma</m>, that is, between <m>z = -1</m> and
          <m>z = 1</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>95\%</m> of values lie between <m>\mu - 2\sigma</m> and
          <m>\mu + 2\sigma</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>99.7\%</m> of values lie between <m>\mu - 3\sigma</m> and
          <m>\mu + 3\sigma</m>.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Almost every value lies within three standard deviations of the
        mean.</p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="ex-empirical-basic">
      <title>Applying the rule</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose <m>X</m> is normal with mean <m>50</m> and standard
        deviation <m>6</m>. Find the three Empirical Rule intervals.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>About <m>68\%</m> lie between <m>50 - 6 = 44</m> and
        <m>50 + 6 = 56</m>.</p>
        <p>About <m>95\%</m> lie between <m>50 - 12 = 38</m> and
        <m>50 + 12 = 62</m>.</p>
        <p>About <m>99.7\%</m> lie between <m>50 - 18 = 32</m> and
        <m>50 + 18 = 68</m>.</p>
        <p>Every one of these is just <m>\mu \pm k\sigma</m>. The Empirical
        Rule needs no table and no technology<mdash/>only arithmetic.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <!-- READING CHECK 5 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-empirical-interval">
      <statement>
        <p>Scores on an entrance exam are approximately normal with
        <m>\mu = 52</m> points and <m>\sigma = 11</m> points. About
        <m>95\%</m> of scores lie between which two values?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>30</m> and <m>74</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. <m>95\%</m> corresponds to two standard deviations:
            <m>52 \pm 2(11)</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>41</m> and <m>63</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is <m>\mu \pm 1\sigma</m>, which captures about
            <m>68\%</m> of scores, not <m>95\%</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>19</m> and <m>85</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is <m>\mu \pm 3\sigma</m>, which captures about
            <m>99.7\%</m> of scores.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>50</m> and <m>54</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>This interval is far too narrow. Each step out from the mean is
            a full standard deviation of <m>11</m> points.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <example xml:id="ex-chile-empirical">
      <title>The Empirical Rule for Chilean heights</title>
      <statement>
        <p>For 15- to 18-year-old Chilean males in 1984<ndash/>1985,
        <m>Y \sim N(172.36, 6.34)</m>. Apply the Empirical Rule.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <table xml:id="table-chile-empirical">
          <title>Empirical Rule intervals for <m>Y \sim N(172.36, 6.34)</m></title>
          <tabular halign="center" top="medium">
            <row bottom="medium" header="yes">
              <cell>Percent</cell>
              <cell>Interval</cell>
              <cell>Heights (cm)</cell>
              <cell><m>z</m>-scores</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>about <m>68\%</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\mu \pm 1\sigma</m></cell>
              <cell><m>166.02</m> to <m>178.70</m></cell>
              <cell><m>-1</m> and <m>1</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>about <m>95\%</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\mu \pm 2\sigma</m></cell>
              <cell><m>159.68</m> to <m>185.04</m></cell>
              <cell><m>-2</m> and <m>2</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="medium">
              <cell>about <m>99.7\%</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\mu \pm 3\sigma</m></cell>
              <cell><m>153.34</m> to <m>191.38</m></cell>
              <cell><m>-3</m> and <m>3</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <!-- READING CHECK 6 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-unusual">
      <statement>
        <p>A 15-year-old Chilean male in 1984 measured <m>193</m> cm.
        Using <xref ref="table-chile-empirical"/>, what can you say?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>He is outside the <m>99.7\%</m> interval, so his height is
            unusual for this population.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. The <m>99.7\%</m> interval ends at <m>191.38</m> cm,
            so fewer than <m>0.3\%</m> of this population is this tall or
            taller.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>He is within the <m>95\%</m> interval, so his height is
            typical.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The <m>95\%</m> interval ends at <m>185.04</m> cm, and
            <m>193 \gt 185.04</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>His height is impossible under a normal distribution.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>A normal distribution assigns nonzero probability to every
            value. Unusual is not the same as impossible.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Nothing, because the Empirical Rule only applies to the
            standard normal distribution.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The Empirical Rule applies to <em>every</em> normal
            distribution<mdash/>that is the point of standardizing.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <conclusion>
    <title>Summary</title>
    <p>A <m>z</m>-score is a standardized value. Standardizing any normal
    distribution produces <m>Z \sim N(0, 1)</m>, with mean <m>0</m> and
    standard deviation <m>1</m>. A <m>z</m>-score reports how many standard
    deviations a value lies above or below its mean, which makes values from
    different distributions directly comparable.</p>
    <p>Standardize a value: <m>z = \dfrac{x - \mu}{\sigma}</m>.</p>
    <p>Recover a value: <m>x = \mu + z\sigma</m>.</p>
    <p>Empirical Rule: about <m>68\%</m>, <m>95\%</m>, and <m>99.7\%</m> of
    values lie within one, two, and three standard deviations of the mean.</p>
    <p>Adapted from <pubtitle>Introductory Statistics</pubtitle>
    (OpenStax<nbsp/>/ Lumen Learning), CC BY 4.0. Chilean height data from the
    London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine (2009).</p>
  </conclusion>
</section>